course: dictionary
lesson: 4550
title: Heath: wild open land
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A heath is a large area of open, wild land where the soil is poor and few trees grow. Look at the words around it for clues. In Toad's list, «the open road, the dusty highway, the heath, the common, the hedgerows», the heath sits among other open places, not in a town or a wood.
explain: Heaths are covered with low plants such as heather, gorse and grasses. «On a hot summer day on the heath you can hear gorse pods pop and fling their seeds out.» Pods that pop out seeds tell you that gorse grows close to the ground, not as tall trees.
explain: Heaths are wild places where creatures live. «Picture a walker on a heath who sees an adder sunbathing on the path: the best plan is simply to stop, step back and go round it.» An adder is a snake, so the walker learns that a heath is home to animals that need space.
fact: A heath is a large area of open, wild land with poor soil and few trees.
fact: Heaths are covered with low plants like heather, gorse and grasses.
fact: Heaths are a home for special plants, insects, lizards, snakes and birds.
fact: Heaths are common in the UK and Ireland.
fact: Heaths can catch fire easily in dry summers.
exercise: «The heath stretched for miles, with no trees in sight.» What is a heath? | A busy road into town | *A large area of open, wild land with few trees | A river with a bridge | A small garden with tall trees
exercise: What covers the ground on a heath? | Water lilies and reeds | *Low plants like heather, gorse and grasses | Tall oak and pine trees | Seaweed and kelp
exercise: When can a heath catch fire easily? | In autumn fog | In spring floods | *In dry summers | During wet winters
test: Which creature from the walker story lives on a heath? | A bumblebee in a garden | *An adder | A pigeon | A goldfish
test: Which picture best shows a heath? | *Wide open land with low plants and few trees | A tall forest with thick trees | A town full of shops | A flat sea with big waves
exam: What is the best plan when you meet an adder on a heath path? | *Stop, step back and go round it | Shout loudly at it | Run as fast as you can past it | Pick it up and move it
